{%- else -%}[((int32_t)({{ expr(index) }})) - 1]
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- A `local` reference is spelled against whichever storage the view says it
    got: `ctx->` names a slot in the enclosing owner's block-context region,
    a bare identifier an object still in the frame OR an arena slot reached
    through the typed pointer of the same name that the owner's entry declared.
    All are the same C lvalue in every other respect, so subscripting is
    shared.

    A region slot that shares an arm overlay with other slots is reached through
    that overlay's member. Which slots share one, and which overlay each landed
    in, is decided in the checked projection; this prints the ordinal it was
    given and takes no view of its own. An arena-placed slot prints bare even
    when it carries an overlay ordinal: the pointer already points at its
    storage, wherever the projection put it. -#}
{%- macro local_storage(value) -%}
{%- if value.context_arena %}{% elif value.context_resident %}ctx->{% if value.context_overlay is not none %}rumoca_galec_arm{{ value.context_overlay }}.{% endif %}{% endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- The identifier itself, with the storage that reaches it. An arena slot's
    entry declaration binds a pointer to the slot's WHOLE array type, so the
    object is spelled `(*name)` and subscripting then proceeds exactly as it
    would on a declared member. Pointing at the whole array rather than at its
    row is what keeps a C compiler's object-size analysis right: `float (*)[3]`
    would say three floats live at that address, and an argument handed from
    there to a `const float[4]` formal is then diagnosed as a read past the end
    of an object that the code never performs. -#}
