{#- The already-decided C indices of an enclosing loop nest, as `[i][j]…`. The
    subscripts here are C indices the emitter chose, not GALEC subscripts, so
    they are printed as-is: the 1-based→0-based conversion `subscript` performs
    has no business running over them a second time. -#}
{%- macro subscript_text(indices) -%}{% for index in indices %}[{{ index }}]{% endfor %}{%- endmacro -%}
{#- Implicit `limit self` at method boundaries (SPEC_0042 T3, SPEC_0034
    GAL-018/GAL-019): declared min/max ranges SATURATE — they are not Modelica
    assertions, nothing is signalled, and a qNaN stays a qNaN. The normative
    behaviour is `rumoca-eval-galec` (`execution.rs::limit_all`, reached from
    `interpreter.rs::invoke`): every INITIALIZED declared block variable
    (interface and protected), arrays elementwise, low bound applied before the
    high bound. These macros mirror that oracle statement for statement. An
    explicit GALEC `limit` statement is a different construct and keeps failing
    closed in the statements macro — T3 covers method boundaries only.

    Two facts this target owns and the oracle does not need:

    * **The numeric domain.** This target executes Real as binary32 and Integer
      as int32 (`c_type`, and the `[integer]` domain in `target.toml`), so a
      declared bound is decided in THAT domain, not in the f64/i64 the checked
      block carries it as. `binary32_bound` / `int32_bound` are the decision:
      each answers `none` when no value of the target type can violate the
      bound (elide the clamp — this is how a Modelica `min =
      -Modelica.Constants.inf` / `max = Modelica.Constants.inf` declaration
      reaches a target that cannot spell `1e60` as a `float`), a literal when
      the bound is representable, and fails closed when it is neither. This
      template picks the domain by naming those filters; the arithmetic is not
      re-derived here against a magic constant.
    * **Determinacy.** The block state is caller-allocated memory with no
      per-slot initialized flag, so the `Startup` RETURN boundary — the one
      boundary that runs before any other method has written anything — clamps
      only what `Startup` is proven to have written
      (`startup.definite_state_writes`). That mirrors the oracle skipping an
      uninitialized slot, and it is what keeps a declared range on a control
      input (which `Startup` never assigns) from making the generated code read
      indeterminate memory. Every later boundary limits the full state. -#}
{%- macro low_bound(variable) -%}
{%- if variable.kind == "real" -%}{% set text = variable.real_min | binary32_bound("min") %}{% if text is not none %}{{ text }}f{% endif %}
{%- elif variable.kind == "integer" -%}{% set text = variable.integer_min | int32_bound("min") %}{% if text is not none %}INT32_C({{ text }}){% endif %}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro high_bound(variable) -%}
{%- if variable.kind == "real" -%}{% set text = variable.real_max | binary32_bound("max") %}{% if text is not none %}{{ text }}f{% endif %}
{%- elif variable.kind == "integer" -%}{% set text = variable.integer_max | int32_bound("max") %}{% if text is not none %}INT32_C({{ text }}){% endif %}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- Nonempty exactly when this variable still saturates in the target domain.
    A declared bound that elided leaves no clamp behind, so a variable whose
    every bound elided is not limited at all. -#}
{%- macro limited(variable) -%}{{ low_bound(variable) }}{{ high_bound(variable) }}{%- endmacro -%}
{#- The one place a boundary scope is spelled: `"all"` is every declaration,
    `"startup"` only the ones `Startup` definitely wrote. -#}
{%- macro in_scope(variable, scope) -%}
{%- if scope != "startup" or variable.name in algorithm_code.package.block.startup.definite_state_writes -%}scoped{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- The declaration ordinals one boundary limits, as a stable text key. The
    startup scope is always a subsequence of `"all"`, so dropping any
    declaration strictly shortens the key: two equal-length keys are the same
    set, and the two boundaries can then share one C function. -#}
{%- macro limited_ordinals(scope) -%}
{%- for variable in algorithm_code.variables -%}
{%- if limited(variable)|length > 0 and in_scope(variable, scope)|length > 0 -%}{{ variable.ordinal }},{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro limiter_name(scope) -%}
{%- if scope == "startup" and limited_ordinals("startup")|length != limited_ordinals("all")|length -%}rumoca_galec_limit_startup{%- else -%}rumoca_galec_limit_self{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro limit_target(variable, indices) -%}
self->{{ symbol(c_state_symbols, variable.name) }}{% for index in indices %}[{{ index }}]{% endfor %}
{%- endmacro -%}
