{#- A method whose body is empty already limited its state on entry, and
    saturation is idempotent, so its return boundary would re-run the identical
    clamp over values nothing has touched. Emit it only when something ran in
    between. `Startup` has no entry boundary at all, so it never uses this. -#}
{%- macro limit_return_boundary(method, indent, scope) -%}
{%- if method.statements|length > 0 %}{{ limit_self_boundary(indent, scope) }}{% endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- Whole-array assignment. A copy of N elements is emitted as ONE call to a
    shared, typed kernel rather than as an open-coded loop, because those are not
    the same verification object: 344 open-coded three-trip loops are 344
    induction variables, 344 bounds and 344 increments a DO-178C reviewer has to
    read, and 344 constructs a MISRA scanner has to analyse, whereas one kernel
    verified once and called 344 times is a single such object. That is the whole
    argument for this section — not line count.

    The kernel is emitted ONLY for a genuine copy: the source has to be an
    array-valued reference whose residual rank matches the loop nest, which is
    exactly the case where the emitted body would have been `t[i] = s[i]` and
    nothing else. A computed source (`(-a[i])`, `a[i] - g`, `sinf(a[i])`, a
    conditional) keeps its loop, because there is no copy there to share.

    Only the INNERMOST dimension becomes a call: the kernel takes a rank-1 run
    and a count, so a rank-2 copy is one loop over the rows and one call per row.
    That is deliberate, and it is the rule EVERY kernel in the shared library
    follows. Parameterising the extent keeps the library at one kernel per
    element type instead of one per (type, rank, extent), and taking the
    operands as rank-1 sidesteps the C99 constraint recorded at
    `function_parameter` — there is no implicit conversion from
    pointer-to-array-of-T to pointer-to-array-of-const-T, so a rank > 1 kernel
    could not have been const-correct on its source without a cast this target
    prohibits. A row of a `float [n][m]` decays to `float *`, which converts to
    `const float *` without one. Handing the kernel `&m[0][0]` and a computed
    flat index would have avoided that too, and is exactly what this target does
    not do: indexing past the end of `m[0]` is out-of-bounds pointer arithmetic
    on the sub-array it addresses, whatever the enclosing object's total size.

    The kernels themselves live in `rumoca_galec_kernels.c`, one library
    translation unit with external linkage, emitted whole beside every model.
    They are NOT `static` copies in this file: several generated blocks linked
    into one application must share one definition, and the reviewed object has
    to be one object.

    The kernel copies FORWARD, element 0 first, which is the order the loop it
    replaces used. That is what makes the substitution value-preserving even when
    the source and the target overlap. -#}
{%- macro copy_kernel_name(scalar) -%}
{%- if scalar is none -%}
{%- elif scalar.kind == "real" -%}rumoca_galec_copy_real
{%- elif scalar.kind == "integer" -%}rumoca_galec_copy_integer
{%- elif scalar.kind == "boolean" -%}rumoca_galec_copy_boolean
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- The kernel call for one rank-1 run: `count`, then the destination run, then
    the source run. Both operands are the enclosing loops' partial subscripting
    of an object whose innermost dimension is `count`. -#}
{#- The fixed-count specializations the emitted kernel library defines
    (`kernels.c.jinja`). A call site whose literal count and Real element type
    match one prints the specialized name and drops the count argument; every
    other site prints the generic counted call. The two spellings execute the
    identical element operations in the identical order, so this is a
    call-emission selection, not a semantic decision. The lists here must
    match the library templates — the emitted `#error` version check ties a
    model source to the kernel header it was generated beside. -#}
{%- set specialized_copy_counts = [3, 15, 24] -%}
{%- set specialized_fill_counts = [2, 3, 15] -%}
{%- set specialized_dot_counts = [6, 12, 15] -%}
{%- set specialized_scaled_add_counts = [2, 3, 6, 12, 15] -%}
{%- macro copy_kernel_call(kernel, count, target_text, source_text, indent) -%}
{%- if kernel == "rumoca_galec_copy_real" and count in specialized_copy_counts -%}
{{ pad(indent) }}{{ kernel }}_{{ count }}({{ target_text }}, {{ source_text }});
{%- else -%}
{{ pad(indent) }}{{ kernel }}(INT32_C({{ count }}), {{ target_text }}, {{ source_text }});
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- The fill kernels, the same idea one operand narrower: a whole-array
    assignment whose every element receives the same literal is a run and a
    value, not a loop. Which assignments those are is decided in the view
    (`uniform_fill_literal`), not here — the value arrives already proven
    uniform and already proven a literal, so the call evaluates it once where
    the loop evaluated it per element and that is not a change. -#}
{%- macro fill_kernel_name(scalar) -%}
{%- if scalar is none -%}
{%- elif scalar.kind == "real" -%}rumoca_galec_fill_real
{%- elif scalar.kind == "integer" -%}rumoca_galec_fill_integer
{%- elif scalar.kind == "boolean" -%}rumoca_galec_fill_boolean
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro fill_kernel_call(kernel, count, target_text, value_text, indent) -%}
{%- if kernel == "rumoca_galec_fill_real" and count in specialized_fill_counts -%}
{{ pad(indent) }}{{ kernel }}_{{ count }}({{ target_text }}, {{ value_text }});
{%- else -%}
{{ pad(indent) }}{{ kernel }}(INT32_C({{ count }}), {{ target_text }}, {{ value_text }});
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{#- A call passes the block context and the operands, and nothing else. The
    callee's outputs are slots in the callee's own region, so the caller reads
    them back by name after the call returns.

    The read-back is where the out pointers used to be, and it happens at the
    same point in the sequence: the callee wrote its outputs before returning
    (previously into its frame, then through the pointers on the way out), and
    the caller's targets are written after the call either way. An operand that
    aliases a target therefore still sees the pre-call value throughout the
    body, exactly as it did.

    The read-back is also where an overlay could go wrong, so note what makes it
    safe. It reads the CALLEE's region and writes the CALLER's, and those are in
    different overlay groups by construction: the caller reaches the callee, so
    the projection's prover refuses to let them share. It is emitted here,
    immediately after the call and inside the same macro, so no sibling call can
    be sequenced between the callee's return and the copy that reads its
    results.

    An output the projection DESTINATION-PLACED has no read-back: the callee's
    slot for it is the caller's own slot, reached through the typed pointer the
    callee's entry declares (`destination_aliases`), so the value is already
    where the copy would have put it. `placed_outputs` carries that decision,
    one flag per output, and the permission behind each one is minted in
    `views::algorithm_code_typed::destination`. That there is nothing between
